\section{Introduction}
Gbesi Gbe (ISO 639-3: gbs; Glottocode: gbes1238) is a Gbe language of southern Benin. Joshua Project places the people mainly in the Atlantique Department (Kpomass\`e, Allada, Tori-Bossito) and lists about 144,000 people. Sources disagree on subgrouping: Wikipedia places it with Ayizo and Kotafon, Joshua Project in an Eastern Gbe cluster. Very little published material was found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Gbe languages (family context): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gbe_languages}
\item Wikipedia, Ayizo language (includes Gbesi in its grouping): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ayizo_language}
\item Glottolog bibliography entry (Angela Kluge 2011); its content was not examined: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/55833}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project, Gbesi Gbe in Benin (reports Scripture translation has started): \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/18818}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found in the search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
No dictionary, primer, or school materials in Gbesi were found. A curriculum would have to be built from scratch, possibly drawing on materials for better-documented neighbouring Gbe languages once their relation to Gbesi is established.

\end{document}
